DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The sseemm__ddeessttrrooyy() function destroys the unnamed semaphore pointed to by
     _s_e_m.  After a successful call to sseemm__ddeessttrrooyy(), _s_e_m is unusable until re-
     initialized by another call to sem_init(3).

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     The sseemm__ddeessttrrooyy() function returns the value 0 if successful; otherwise
     the value -1 is returned and the global variable _e_r_r_n_o is set to indicate
     the error.

EERRRROORRSS
     The sseemm__ddeessttrrooyy() function will fail if:

     [EINVAL]           The _s_e_m argument points to an invalid semaphore.

     [EBUSY]            There are currently threads blocked on the semaphore
                        that _s_e_m points to.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     sem_init(3)

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
     The sseemm__ddeessttrrooyy() function conforms to ISO/IEC 9945-1:1996 (“POSIX.1”).

     POSIX does not define the behavior of sseemm__ddeessttrrooyy() if called while there
     are threads blocked on _s_e_m, but this implementation is guaranteed to re‐
     turn -1 and set _e_r_r_n_o to EBUSY if there are threads blocked on _s_e_m.

